[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from October 3rd, 2020 titled "What exactly do
[00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.520]   you mean by anal?" Thoughts on leadership and self-awareness. I remember one time having an
[00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.840]   argument that went like this, Joe, I think there's enough detail in there. Dave, no, there's not.
[00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.160]   There's no underpinnings, there's no rigor in the thought process. Remember, David Ogilvy always
[00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:22.960]   said "good writing is slavery" and ergo you need to dive deep and "Joe, oh, you can be so anal."
[00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.800]   Dave, I don't think I'm being anal, I'm just being rigorous. Joe, yes you are.
[00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.200]   Dave, well, what exactly do you mean by anal? I always try to listen to myself and once in a
[00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.600]   while I have a "did I just say that" moment. Did I just say "what exactly do you mean by anal?"
[00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:40.480]   Oh shit, I did. Isn't that kind of the definition of being anal? Oh shit, it is. Heck, Dave,
[00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.280]   you may as well just have replied "what I really want to know is, is there a hyphen in anal-retentive?"
[00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.760]   The actual issue here is one of leadership. Being aware of your strengths and weaknesses,
[00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.840]   trying to avoid overdoing your strengths and working to compensate for your weaknesses.
[00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.920]   It's critical that all leaders focus on this because, by default, most folks will
[00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.160]   overplay to their strengths to a fault, effectively turning them into weaknesses and ignore their
[00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:07.600]   weaknesses. It's not hard to be self-aware when it comes to most strengths and weaknesses. Most
[00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.600]   folks know, for example, if they're great at public speaking and bad at financial analysis,
[00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.320]   or great at individual problem solving but bad in groups, or high on IQ but low on EQ.
[00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.840]   People usually know. Sometimes we euphemize with ourselves. For example, while others might say
[00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.320]   I'm "detail-oriented," I prefer "rigorous," "blunt," I prefer "direct," "contrarian,"
[00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.800]   I prefer "critical thinking," and so on. But at least you're circling the same pond. You have
[00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.120]   awareness of the area, though you might soften how you think about it to protect the old ego,
[00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.120]   relative to how others might more bluntly, or should I say directly, describe it.
[00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:44.080]   But some weaknesses are harder to self-assess. For example, I've taken assessments that basically
[00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.800]   prove I'm low on flexibility. But I never knew it. In fact, I thought I was supremely flexible
[00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.120]   because I was capable of moving. Think, okay, we'll move a bit in your direction. You see,
[00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.880]   I'm flexible. Voila. QED. Bravo, chef. I was, however, blind to the fact that one person's
[00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.280]   mile is another's inch. When you're inflexible, you risk self-congratulation for a tidbit of
[00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.960]   demonstrated movement when the other party thinks you haven't moved when the other party thinks you
[00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.920]   haven't moved at all. As another example, because communication is one of my strengths, I always
[00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.640]   thought I did better in groups, when in fact I do better with people one-to-one, which was a key
[00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.440]   strength of which I wasn't even aware. Some of these things are just hard to see. My advice on
[00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.480]   this front is threefold. Be aware of your strengths, and beware your natural tendency to overplay to
[00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.040]   them. If one of your strengths has become a running joke, for example, at one point one of
[00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.560]   my staff handed out Captain Anal pins, it could be time to think about it. Be aware of your weaknesses,
[00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:41.120]   and, while you can work on them if you want, use building a complementary team as your primary way
[00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:46.960]   to compensate. Attend programs like LDP managers, directors, or LAPC levels to build a deep
[00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.200]   understanding of both. These programs aren't cheap, but they will give you self-awareness
[00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.720]   in a kind of data-driven and ergo-virtual. Why undeniable way that few other programs will,
[00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.320]   and can somebody please spellcheck this thing to make sure there aren't any errors?
