[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.040]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from April 23rd, 2023, titled "Don't
[00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:07.640]   Hide Behind Ending ARR."
[00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:12.680]   I'm writing to propose that we limit discussion of my top, pet peep, SaaS metric, ending ARRR.
[00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:16.920]   Wait, but aren't you the guy who said that if you only knew two things about a SaaS company
[00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:21.400]   and needed to value it, one would be ending ARR and the other would be its growth rate?
[00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.000]   Yes, that's true.
[00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.760]   But the primary business of a SaaS company isn't valuing itself, and, as an operational
[00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.300]   metric, ending ARRR stinks.
[00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.760]   I dislike talking about ending ARR for the same reason I dislike talking about revenue.
[00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:38.040]   In a SaaS company, revenue is a result, not a driver.
[00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.980]   Revenue is a math problem, not a key performance indicator, KPI.
[00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:45.840]   The same is true for ending ARR, it's a math problem, just a simpler one.
[00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.920]   Let's use an example to show my point.
[00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.280]   Imagine you're at a post-quarter board meeting and one of the executives presents this leaky
[00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.940]   bucket along with this narrative, blah blah blah, well it was a good quarter, we landed
[00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:57.320]   at 96% of plan, blah blah blah.
[00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.200]   How does that narrative make me feel?
[00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.200]   Generally angry.
[00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.200]   How angry?
[00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.640]   Well, that depends a lot on who's saying it.
[00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.580]   If it's the VP of New Customer Sales, then very angry.
[00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.980]   They landed at 60% of plan, not 96%.
[00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:13.380]   If it's the VP of Sales responsible for all new ARR, then still pretty angry.
[00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.640]   They landed at 73% of plan, not 96%.
[00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.360]   If it's the VP of Customer Success and they're responsible only for churn, then not angry
[00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:21.360]   at all.
[00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.400]   They were spot on plan though, we had a little more shrinkage, ARR and a little less lost
[00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:26.800]   ARR than plan.
[00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.660]   Good job, but I have a few questions.
[00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:33.080]   If it's the CRO responsible for both new and churn ARR, then back to very angry.
[00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.280]   Net new ARR, new ARR, ARR.
[00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.960]   Churn ARR was $825,000, 60% of plan, not 96%.
[00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:41.760]   Look, bad quarters happen.
[00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.800]   I'm not angry about the bad quarter, I'm angry when people try to pretend a bad quarter
[00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:47.080]   was a good one.
[00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:50.920]   Or even more scarily, at the prospect that someone might actually believe that a bad quarter
[00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:55.960]   was a good one, talking about ending ARR is like a giant "hey look over here" distraction.
[00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.840]   It's the green arrow that I added above.
[00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.980]   Executives should talk about their area of responsibility and characterize their quarter
[00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.040]   based on performance in that area.
[00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:09.760]   When a VP of Sales who's at 60% of plan talks about a good quarter on ending ARR, I ask
[00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.520]   myself, when did they get promoted to CFO?
[00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:17.400]   When a CRO who's at 73% of plan says, "As shareholders, we should be happy that we grew
[00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:23.400]   the ending, ARR 67%, year over year," I think, "No, as shareholders, we pay you to hit the
[00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.240]   new ARR plan and you're at 73%."
[00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.660]   When it comes to sales leaders, ending ARR, like patriotism, is the last refuge of the
[00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:31.660]   scoundrel.
[00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.960]   The CEO and CFO can talk about ending ARR.
[00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.640]   But even they need to get the delicate narrative right, remembering that for a SaaS company
[00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.920]   at the above scale, it's all about acquiring new customers to join your NRR expansion flywheel.
[00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.760]   Here's the right narrative.
[00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.300]   Overall it was a weak quarter.
[00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.200]   We landed at 73% of the new ARR plan.
[00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:56.240]   While we got close on expansion, ARR at 93%, new logo ARR was a dismal 60% of plan, something
[00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.020]   we're going to drill into with Kelly in the next section.
[00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:00.880]   On the churn side, things were pretty good.
[00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:05.240]   We hit the churn target of $625,000 and while we were able to beat plan on lost customer
[00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:10.520]   ARR, we had $50,000 more in shrinkage ARR than plan, which Reese will discuss.
[00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:15.200]   The net result is that we ended the quarter at 96% of ending ARR, a gap of 500 and fixed
[00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.060]   percent of ending ARR, a gap of $530,000.
[00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:21.140]   We think we can close in 2Q.
[00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:23.080]   Why is this the right narrative?
[00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:26.880]   It talks about by performance by area, where action and accountability happen, and not
[00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:27.880]   in aggregate.
[00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:29.040]   It's transparent.
[00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.860]   It doesn't pretend a bad performance is a good one, or that 93% of plan is good.
[00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.640]   It tees up subsequent discussion by the relevant leaders.
[00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.240]   Trust me, leading that discussion is a form of accountability all by itself.
[00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:43.700]   It discusses ending ARR correctly, both as a result and as a cumulative metric, which
[00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:47.900]   means that, unlike the other period metrics, it's one that we should strive to re-catch.
[00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.960]   The last point is subtle.
[00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:54.480]   Instead of using ARR as a mathematical keel to damp you (under performance by a factor
[00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.720]   of around 6, we're doing the opposite), we're recognizing that even if we hit every other
[00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.040]   plan number for the rest of year, that we will still end the year with $500,000 shortfall.
[00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:06.440]   We're recognizing that and making a commitment to try and catch back up.
[00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:10.160]   We're using ending ARR to increase accountability, not dampen it.
[00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:11.160]   Goosebumps.
[00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.240]   Hopefully, this explains my modest proposal unless you're the CEO or CFO and it's the
[00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:19.280]   finance section of the meeting, you should never talk about ending ARR.
[00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:21.080]   Talk about what drives it instead.
