[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.240]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from August 26, 2024 titled "The Four Key
[00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.920]   Questions About Every Angel Investment Opportunity". Excluding VC funds, I directly invested in a few
[00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.000]   dozen startups. While the jury's still out on my performance, I can share a few things that I've
[00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.080]   learned along the way. This isn't easy. Though I'd never thought I'd say this, it's given me some
[00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.800]   empathy for early-stage venture capitalists. Be prepared for 10-year-plus exit horizons and
[00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.840]   to lose every penny you put in. I mock VCs a little less. It's easy to mock VCs for being too
[00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.320]   fashion-driven, too pedigree-oriented, and too herd-like in their behavior. But when I'm actually
[00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.720]   in their shoes evaluating seed stage opportunities, I see myself adopting some of these very same
[00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:44.400]   behaviors. Think, it's an AI data play, the founders dropped out of Stanford PhDs, and DCVC
[00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.840]   is leading the round? I'm in. S everything. You can't invest in a deal that you never see.
[00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.840]   This leads me to believe that you should only consider angel investing if you have access to
[00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.760]   amazing group of people who you think will do great things in the future, for example,
[00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.520]   by having worked together at a startup seemingly destined to spawn many more.
[00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.600]   I can see the power law at work. While I have confidence that my returns will be quite good in
[00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.760]   the end and marking them to market today is largely meaningless, I can already see that
[00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.320]   they will be concentrated in a few superstar investments. I learned one of my more impactful,
[00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:20.480]   Li El, sans at an A16Z event a few years back. After the presentation, I went up to one of the
[00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:25.360]   partners and we spoke. DK, hey, I was looking at XYZCo the other day thought you might be interested
[00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.720]   in taking a look at them. A 16Z partner, yes, we know them. I recently had a few team members do
[00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.640]   a full rundown on that space, there are about a dozen companies in it, and we decided that while
[00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:38.320]   we liked the space, they weren't the team we wanted to bet on, and a light bulb went off.
[00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:42.880]   When I find in seed stage deal, I evaluate the founder, the story, the product, and then make a
[00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.440]   decision. When professionals find a seed stage deal, they put a team of three MBAs on finding
[00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.000]   and evaluating every startup in the emerging space. That's a big difference, I never know if
[00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.880]   they're the best team in the space. I can only know if I think they're impressive. This observation
[00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.000]   backed me into what I now use as my four question framework for evaluating angel opportunities.
[00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.600]   Why this idea? Do people need this? Will they pay for it? How big can it be? Is this really a company
[00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:13.840]   or just a feature? Why now? Is this idea too far ahead of its time? Or is this company too late?
[00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.560]   Why this company? Do they have a technological head start? Do they have uncommon expertise that
[00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:23.280]   is difficult or impossible to replicate? Why this leadership team? Why is this particular
[00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:28.080]   group of smart people, hint, they're all smart, going to win? What is special about the founders
[00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.760]   that makes me believe they will beat everyone else, and/or change the race as need be?
[00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:38.080]   I know many such frameworks, Xi. Saint. I'm not making any unique misclaim on this approach.
[00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.320]   I am saying, however, that this is my current framework when wearing my angel investor hat,
[00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:45.680]   and I think it's really useful for founders to switch perspectives from time to time
[00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.200]   so they can get a better sense for what the people on the other side of the table are thinking.
[00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.000]   And, in the end, they're thinking like investors. The more you can too, the better you'll do when
[00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.880]   fundraising. This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI with permission from Dave.
