[00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.880]   exercise the second option grant. "You can't," she said. "It's been cancelled." "Why?" I said,
[00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.960]   watching a G-Wagon disappear before my eyes. "Because you discontinued your service to the
[00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.520]   company." "No, I didn't." "You just stopped calling. That's the nature of advisory work.
[00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.840]   You call, and I answer." This launched a multi-week argument about if and when I'd
[00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.320]   stopped providing services and many questions about why I'd not been notified of any termination,
[00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.840]   so I'd have at least known the 90-day option exercise window had started.
[00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.480]   The dispute eventually escalated to the GC who, in firing me, started my 90-day clock,
[00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:13.760]   admitting the option was still valid. Why do I tell you this story? First, because it's hilarious.
[00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.440]   The GC could easily just have said he'd honored the option but instead chose to communicate that
[00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.600]   good news by firing me. Second, because this is what happens when you use paperwork written
[00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.000]   for employees to engage with advisors. In this post, I'll share what I've learned over the past 15
[00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.000]   years advising startups to give you a better idea of what to do when someone asks you to advise their
[00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.400]   startup. See my disclaimers, remember that this is not legal or financial advice and that you
[00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.720]   should hire professionals to get advice specific to your situation. When asked to be an advisor
[00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:43.280]   of an early-stage company, I think you should do four things. Decide if you want to advise the
[00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.800]   company, discuss, and set clear expectations on both sides. Agree on compensation, which is
[00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:54.320]   typically, but not always, stock. Put a good, quick legal agreement in place. Decide if you
[00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.040]   want to advise the company. This may seem like an obvious first step, but sometimes the answer
[00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:04.240]   really should be no. There are lots of good reasons to advise a company. For example, you can
[00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:09.520]   build your network with the company's team and investors, learn by seeing more business situations,
[00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:15.120]   sow seeds for potential downstream relationships, for example, employee or board, pay it forward.
[00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.920]   But there are also some good reasons not to advise a company, including bad chemistry.
[00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.440]   Life's too short. Avoid deals where the investors are trying to impose you as an
[00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.680]   advisor to a reluctant executive. Poor fit. You want to be asked about problems you've
[00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.520]   seen dozens of times. You'll give better answers and it will take less of your time
[00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.360]   when they have a one-shot problem. Advising is a long-term relationship game.
[00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:40.800]   If a company just needs help with one thing, for example, positioning, reorganization,
[00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.720]   everyone could be better off if you do a cash consulting deal or just offer some input for free.
[00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:48.720]   Restrictive terms in the advisor agreement, such as non-solicitatio,
[00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.720]   n) no hire or non-compete agreements. Once you're advising company A, you may discover that you wish
[00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.800]   you were advising company B. While switching courses may or may not be prohibited under the
[00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.760]   agreement, it's always bad form and bad for your reputation. This is now the top reason I decline
[00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.320]   advisory deals. There is an opportunity cost associated with advising that many overlook.
[00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:13.280]   Discuss and agree on clear expectations. I don't think this needs to be contractual,
[00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.680]   or even necessarily written down since most agreements feature bilateral termination for
[00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.880]   convenience anyway, but I do think it's important to have a detailed conversation about expectations.
[00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:27.680]   These are all great topics to include in that conversation. The specific areas where the
[00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.080]   company wants help. For me, that might be SaaS metrics, marketing, board presentations,
[00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.560]   and CEO mentoring. The form of the help. Some people want you to build their financial model.
[00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.800]   I just want to provide feedback to your finance head who can then iterate and improve it themselves.
[00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.040]   Interaction frequency. For me, that's typically measured in times per month.
[00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.600]   In-office attendance. Some of my friends like to show up once/week and have water cooler
[00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:53.680]   encounters to really get to know the company and its scuttlebutt. Me, not so much.
[00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.920]   Periodic versus ad hoc meetings. K, to meet, because there's something to discuss,
[00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:02.160]   not because it's Tuesday. But different people see this differently, and compensation structure
[00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.440]   impacts this as well. Time commitment. Some people like to agree on hours/month, I don't.
[00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.520]   When you buy experience, you're paying for fast answers, so hours is the wrong metric.
[00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.960]   Value is measured by how quickly I can solve a problem, not how slowly.
[00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:20.560]   Agree on compensation. Because I'm talking about early stage, for example, seed/pre-seed advisory,
[00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.080]   the typical company doesn't have much cash and wants to pay advisors in stock,
[00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.320]   typically measured in the tenths of a percent. Let's do some sample math which will rely on
