[00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:56.640]   hourly rate, $49 you might see the $500,000 here and think amazing money, and you'd be right.
[00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:02.720]   In the unlikely case, this actually happens. In a more realistic $500 million exit scenario,
[00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.560]   those shares might be worth $250,000 due to debt and prefer.
[00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:10.960]   Enzes. In a dire scenario, for example, with multiple liquidation preferences and a large
[00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:16.080]   amount of capital raised, they might be worth $0. Note that this example assumes no debt,
[00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:20.320]   no participating preferred shares, and an exit value that clears the preference stack by enough
[00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:25.040]   that all investors take their pro-rata ownership instead of their preferences. So, to go back to
[00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:30.160]   the point, the majority of these deals pay $0 to the advisor. When you average out the occasional
[00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:36.320]   big hit across the rest, you might end up making $150/hour. And the cash flow is lumpy. It's a
[00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:41.440]   fun hobby, but it's no way to make a living. So, when evaluating an advisory opportunity,
[00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.080]   do the math and run some scenarios. Then think about the commitment you're willing to make.
[00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.160]   And, more than anything, do the work because it's fun and you grow by doing it,
[00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:55.200]   not because you're banking on any big hit. If you consider any compensation whatsoever as upside,
[00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:00.320]   you'll always be happy, and maybe sometimes when you get lucky, very happy. Put a good,
