[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from September 7, 2024, titled "We Are Not the
[00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.280]   Same, The Obligatory Post on Founder Mode." Updated September 23 to include link to a Brian
[00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.920]   Chesky interview discussing Founder Mode. Founder Mode became all the rage last week,
[00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:20.640]   following a Brian Chesky speech whose contents are seemingly not available on mine and a Paul Graham
[00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.120]   blog post about that speech. Since everyone's weighing in on Founder Mode and a few long-term
[00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:29.200]   readers have specifically asked for my take, that will be the subject of this post.
[00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.520]   Founder Mode is defined in opposition to Manager Mode, the conventional wisdom as taught in
[00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.240]   business schools, about how to manage an organization. The basic idea is that B-School
[00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:43.840]   teaches managers to delegate and empower, lest they be guilty of micromanagement. But, in Founder Mode,
[00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.080]   founders dive heroically into details penetrating the gaslighting of their value,
[00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.960]   destroying C-level execs which include, and this is an actual quote, "some of the most skillful
[00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.480]   liars in the world." Let me start by saying I have a 270+ degree view on this problem.
[00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.760]   I advise and sit on boards, so a well-meaning advice giver. I've been the CEO of two startups,
[00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.320]   and I've also been one of those presumably duplicitous C-level execs. But the one thing
[00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:13.200]   I've not been is a founder. I've worked with plenty of them. I've been appointed CEO of a
[00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.000]   company with a founder staying on the team for years alongside me. Without restating the article
[00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.720]   or the debate about it, let's just cut directly to my take. First, we are not the same. It's an
[00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.680]   excellent point and one that needs stating. Now, usually I cover it from the other side.
[00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.880]   And I've gotten myself into, at times, deep trouble by not recognizing these differences.
[00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.760]   As a professional manager, you have less moral authority to drive change. It's not your baby
