[00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:56.880]   Here's what we'll cover.
[00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.320]   How we got here, a brief, salient history of SaaS Metrics.
[00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.960]   When SaaS Metrics break, for example, when monthly spend varies or contracts are prepaid,
[00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:12.560]   how ARR has become the Achilles heel of SaaS Metrics, a big problem given that virtually all top SaaS Metrics depend on ARR.
[00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.440]   How proxies for ARR have emerged as a result.
[00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:22.000]   How and why public companies don't generally report ARR, and how analysts use implied ARR instead.
[00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.400]   The four impacts of ARR instead.
[00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.800]   The four impacts of AI on SaaS Metrics, specifically, one rethinking ARR,
[00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.920]   one vesty, three rethinking pricing model drivers, and 33 rethinking pricing model drivers.
[00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.560]   The heavy lifting will move to the pricing model.
[00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.240]   A quick case study on Piper.
[00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:46.160]   The impressive AISDR from Qualified. How she is priced. And some nuances in estimating the value she delivers when you hire her.
[00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:49.120]   The presentation will feature a brief moment of null and collie.
[00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.240]   A reflection back on the history of software gross margins.
[00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.440]   The best aside will be when I present my three rules of pricing.
[00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.480]   You can register to attend SaaS Metrics Palooza here.
[00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:02.240]   My session is at 9/10am Pacific Time on Tuesday, October 8th.
[00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.800]   I will do a separate post after the presentation with a link to the video and a link to the slides.
