[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.840]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from November 7, 2024 titled "Should Marketers
[00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.520]   Say Business Outcomes?" Sometimes. But make sure it's not out of laziness or ignorance.
[00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.840]   Increasingly, you'll hear the term "business outcomes" popping up in software marketing.
[00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.240]   While I initially loathed the phrase for its vagueness, over time I've come to believe that,
[00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.960]   much like the frequently abused word "solutions," there is a right and a wrong way to use business
[00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:31.280]   outcomes. Let's start with two examples. Example one, our skills. Based HR solution helps you
[00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.120]   inventory your talent so you can better leverage the people you have and achieve the business
[00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.240]   outcomes you desire. Example two, our metadata platform helps you deliver clean data to business
[00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.480]   analysts so they can build better models, make better decisions using them, and deliver superior
[00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:50.160]   business outcomes. These are ostensibly similar claims. Both are in feature/advantage/benefit
[00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.840]   form, that is, our feature X gives you advantage, Y that delivers benefit Z. However, I strongly
[00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:01.280]   dislike the first one, while I'm almost okay with the second. Y. Example one deals with a specific
[00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:06.160]   application where it's possible to enumerate concrete benefits such as save money by making
[00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:11.280]   more productive use of your existing talent, save more money by eliminating new hires from the plan,
[00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.920]   increase employee satisfaction by providing more stimulating work that leverages their skills,
[00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.960]   reduce attrition and back low costs as a result of increased employee satisfaction. Saying business
[00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.200]   outcomes instead of enumerating these advantages strikes me as either lazy if you can't be
[00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.760]   troubled to enumerate them or ignorant if you don't know them. It leaves too much to the mind
[00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.320]   of the reader. The reader has to figure out the advantages of the feature. They have to answer.
[00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.640]   The question, "So what?" on their own. Leaving too much to the mind of the reader by omitting
[00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:44.000]   advantages and benefits is, not to put too fine a point on it, the cardinal sin of marketing.
[00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.560]   Don't make people figure it out. Tell them. If laundry detergents can take you from the green
[00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:53.360]   spot, feature, which is an emulsifier to remove stains, function to wider towels advantage,
[00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.240]   to happier spouse first order so, what advantage, to kiss from your spouse, ultimate benefit,
[00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.600]   then we in technology can certainly take you from skills inventories, feature,
[00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.640]   to happier employees advantage, to saving money advantage, to getting promoted, ultimate benefit.
[00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:10.080]   When you have something to work with, when you have an advantage is stacked to climb,
[00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.240]   failing to do so is a dereliction of marketing duty. You should be called before the marketing
[00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.320]   high tribunal, convicted, and sentenced to 10 years of writing technical documentation.
[00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:23.760]   I'd argue this is often caused by a subtle form of templatitis, an endemic disease in marketing,
[00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.200]   where you fill in the template with the name of the template field. In this case,
[00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.480]   business outcomes. Example 2 suffers from the same problem, but with one big difference. Because
[00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:36.880]   we're marketing a low-level data platform that can be used for almost anything, it's basically
[00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.280]   impossible to enumerate benefits without knowing more about what the customer wants to do with it.
[00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.720]   If I were writing a solutions piece on customer relationship management,
