[00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.040]   it will help advance you on your strategic mission. I remind them that better data means
[00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.440]   better models means better models means better data means better models means better models means
[00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:03.600]   better nayses means better decisions means better business outcomes. We're seeking,
[00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.240]   it's hard to do better. Other than being generic, for example, more profit, which isn't compelling
[00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:12.880]   or taking shots in the dark, for example, increase inventory turns that will either hit or miss.
[00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:16.960]   But since I know that most of our customers are delivering data to finance, marketing,
[00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:21.680]   and operations, I throw that out to be a bit more specific. Think, ask me about how we help
[00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:26.640]   finance teams. As with many challenging games, sometimes the only winding move is not to play,
[00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.760]   or to change the rules. How can we do that? Write a different piece. Sure, every product needs its
[00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:37.680]   webpage and product overview. And that's the land of generic feature/advantage/benefit marketing.
[00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.240]   Climb the benefits stack as high as you can. But since it's hard to get strong business benefits
[00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.720]   when playing the generic game, maybe you should invest less energy in product marketing and more
[00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.520]   in solution marketing. Write about use cases instead. Write a solutions piece about how your
[00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.480]   metadata system can help customers build their customer data platform, CDP, and get the many
[00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:02.000]   marketing benefits of a CDP. Write a case study about how BigBank uses your met, a data platform
[00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.360]   to help with fraud detection, and save $60 million/year as a result. But either way,
[00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:12.160]   don't get lazy and say business outcomes, because you don't want to enumerate them, or worse yet,
[00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:16.560]   because you don't know them. Check yourself each time you write the words. Ask yourself,
[00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.440]   "Can I climb the stack higher? Can I enumerate actual benefits? Am I truly in a situation where
[00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.560]   business outcomes is the best I can do?" If yes, say it, and then start thinking about the next piece
[00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:31.440]   you need to write so you can change the rules. This audio version was created by Grant Duncan
